<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luso-Africans</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luso-Africans were communities of mixed Portuguese and African descent, or Africans of Portuguese culture, who arose on the coasts of West and West-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> from the 15th century. Many descended from lançados, Portuguese traders who settled among African peoples, and they acted as brokers in the trade in slaves, wax, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and cloth along rivers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casamance">Casamance</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacheu">Cacheu</a>. They spoke Portuguese-based <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creoles</a> and were often Catholic.</p></div>
